[standards/application/frontend/STD-APP-FE-001-react-application-standards.md#std-fe-007-500kb-gzipped-per-exposed-module]

#### STD-FE-007: ≤500KB Gzipped Per Exposed Module

**MUST NOT** produce an exposed module whose **chunk graph** exceeds **500KB gzipped** when loaded. The budget is measured **per `exposes` entry** — the total JavaScript downloaded when `container.get('./ComponentName')` is called, excluding shared singletons (`react`, `react-dom`, `react-router-dom`).

> **Why per exposed module, not per package:** A single MF remote may expose multiple components from one `remoteEntry.js`. The container bootstrap is tiny; each component's chunks load lazily on demand. Measuring the package total would unfairly penalise multi-component packages — what matters is how much JavaScript loads for each individual component when it is mounted on a page.

CI **MUST** measure and fail the build on budget violations. Use `webpack-bundle-analyzer` to inspect per-component chunk graphs:

```bash
npm install --save-dev webpack-bundle-analyzer
```

```javascript
// webpack.config.js — add bundle analysis
const { BundleAnalyzerPlugin } = require('webpack-bundle-analyzer');

if (process.env.ANALYZE) {
  plugins.push(new BundleAnalyzerPlugin({ analyzerMode: 'static', reportFilename: 'dist/bundle-report.html' }));
}
```

For single-component packages, a standard `bundlesize` check on `dist/*.js` is sufficient. For multi-component packages, use the bundle analyzer report to verify each exposed module's chunk graph independently — `bundlesize` on `dist/*.js` would measure individual chunk files, not the full chunk graph per component.

```json
// .bundlesizerc.json — single-component packages only
{
  "files": [
    { "path": "dist/*.js", "maxSize": "500 kB" }
  ]
}
```

